sentences.l:
And having received me, that man in his high halls
kindly loved me, as if a father his own son
come after a long time, newly, from elsewhere;

but he then came into his own lair,
and upon both of them sent an unseemly doom;
so will Odysseus send an unseemly doom upon those men.

He said he saw him on an island, strong pains having,
in the halls of the nymph Calypso, who by constraint
holds him;

that Odysseus truly is already in his fatherland,
sitting or creeping, learning of these evil deeds,
and for all the suitors evil he is planting;

But when it was meal-time, and the flocks had come up
from all sides from the farms, and they drove them who did so before,
then indeed to them spoke Medon:

“Stranger, since indeed then to the town to go you are eager
today, as my lord ordered — truly I, for my part,
would wish you here to be left as protector of the steading;

and he to the town led his lord,
like to a pitiful beggar and to an old man,
leaning on a staff;
